        "explanation": "Both images are PCA scatter plots of the samples, colored by iClusterPlus cluster membership (clusters 1, 2 and 3), with a similar number of samples (about 85\u201390). Both meet the task: three iClusterPlus subtypes shown on a PCA plot with points colored by cluster.\n\nHow they differ:\n1. **What the PCA was run on.** The reference PCA seems to use the original high-dimensional data, since PC1 runs from about \u221260 to 60 and PC2 from about \u221250 to 45. The candidate runs PCA on the two iClusterPlus latent variables (K=2). That is why its PC1 (67.8%) and PC2 (32.2%) add up to 100%, and why its axis ranges are smaller (about \u221225 to 27 and \u221216 to 15).\n2. **How well the clusters separate.** In the reference, the clusters overlap a lot. Cluster 2 forms two groups in the upper left and upper right, and clusters 1 and 3 are mixed through the middle and bottom. In the candidate, the three clusters are cleanly separated: cluster 3 on the left, cluster 1 on the right and upper area, and cluster 2 in the lower middle. So the two plots give different messages about how distinct the subtypes are.\n3. **Cluster sizes.** These differ. The reference has about 40 / 30 / 18 samples in clusters 1 / 2 / 3. The candidate has about 27 / 24 / 33. This means the cluster assignments themselves are different, not just the labels.\n\nDifferences in colors, titles and axis labels are acceptable under the evaluation guideline. However, the different PCA input, different separation pattern and different cluster sizes mean the plots do not show the same data. They match on task and plot type but not on the specific results, so I rate them partially equivalent."
